RING_OF_NESTOR_", "content": "A group of Late Minoan signet rings fashioned in 
precious metals and engraved with complex and evocative iconographic schemes 
appears to depict ‘nature’ or ‘rural’ cults enacted at extra-urban sanctuaries,
and may have functioned as inalienable possessions implicated in the expression
and maintenance of elite identities during the Aegean Bronze Age. The images on
the ring bezels depict human figures in association with epiphanic figures 
situated in settings characterised by the presence of [...] 1.   The text 
explores the authenticity of the 'Ring of Nestor' through iconographic 
analysis.\n2.   Contradictions in technical analysis highlight the importance 
of iconography in Aegean studies.\n3.   The connection between incurved altars 
and the ring's depiction supports its authenticity.\n4.   The ring's 
iconography may reflect cultural practices from the 1920s.\n5.   Generations of
scholars have debated the genuineness of both Nestor's and Minos' rings. [...] 
This paper presents an analysis of the iconography surrounding the 'Ring of 
Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates. By 
revisiting key iconographic features and comparisons, the paper aims to 
contribute to the ongoing discourse regarding the ring's significance in Minoan
and Mycenaean art. Observations draw upon historical scholarship and recent 
discoveries to question and affirm previous conclusions related to the ring's 
motifs and their implications.", "raw_content_chars": 20000, "published_at": 
