ring had become an object of considerable artistry. Gold bezels were finely 
engraved with mythological and ritual scenes. The so-called Ring of Nestor — a 
large gold signet acquired near Pylos, whose authenticity was long debated but 
has been increasingly supported by recent scholarship — bears an elaborate 
depiction interpreted by scholars as a scene of the underworld across its broad
bezel. The four gold [...] In 2015, archaeologists Jack Davis and Sharon 
Stocker of the University of Cincinnati, excavating near the Palace of Nestor 
at Pylos in the Greek Peloponnese, uncovered an intact, unlooted shaft grave 
containing the remains of a Bronze Age warrior. He had lain undisturbed for 
some three and a half thousand years. Among the grave goods — weapons, gold, 
ivory, and precious stones — were four gold signet rings, their bezels finely 
engraved with Minoan ritual scenes: priestesses, bull-jumping, [...] The signet
ring is defined by its bezel: the flat or slightly domed face that carries the 
engraved device. This is the element that separates a signet from every other 
category of ring. Everything else serves the bezel.", "raw_content_chars": 
