Evans, Arthur. 1925. “‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan 
After-World and A Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia.” The Journal of Hellenic Studies 45, no. 1: 1-75.  Evely, 
Doniert. 2012. “Material Crafts: Materials and Industries,” in E. Cline (ed.), 
The Oxford Handbook of the Bronze Age Aegean, Oxford University Press, 387-404.
[...] Additional evidence may be found in the Ring of Nestor, discovered on the
southwestern coast of the Peloponnese near Pylos, which depicts a scene 
containing lepidopterans in the upper left quadrant of the image (Fig. 1).4 The
ring itself is only 3.5 by 2.2 cm, making the lepidopterans incredibly small 
compared to the Archanes’ signet ring, which is similar in size but has much 
more prominent lepidopterans. These two lepidopterans are very similar in style
to those found on the ring from [...] on the ring from Archanes, as well as 
appearing in a similar cultic scene of elaborately dressed women conversing 
with raised arms. Evans, in his initial description of the ring, was incredibly
confident in the identification of these figures as butterflies due to the 4 
The Ring of Nestor is mentioned in spite of suspicion that it may be a forgery 
largely due to it aiding the paper’s thesis. The thesis is not harmed whether 
the ring is real or not, so it is mentioned in the case that it is", 
