afterlife domain and described a human couple entering the Elysian Fields.[4] A
reference note in the supplied material associates the ring with a seated 
goddess and a companion, but the referenced figure and surrounding description 
are not available in full.[5] A lower-credibility modern account calls the 
bezel depiction elaborate and characterizes it as an underworld scene; because 
that account does not provide a detailed image analysis or named authorities, 
the description should be treated as a secondary summary rather than a 
definitive physical description.[6]\n\nA later iconographic proposal by A. G. 
Vlachopoulos, an archaeologist whose work concerns Aegean iconography, 
identifies the motif previously called Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” as a 
scorpion. In that interpretation the scorpion is an apotropaic symbol that 
kills the “Minoan Dragon” of the ring.[7] The accessible abstract does not 
include the image analysis supporting the identification, so the scorpion 
reading remains an attributed interpretation rather than an uncontested 
observation. The evidence likewise does not establish whether the bezel design 
should technically be called engraved, carved, raised, or relief 
