"severity": "hard", "report": "# Ring of Nestor\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is the 
conventional name for a Cretan–Mycenaean gold signet ring identified in the 
supplied scholarly literature as CMS VI, no. 277. Its modern importance derives
from the combination of its elaborate bezel imagery, its association with 
Minoan and Mycenaean Bronze Age art, and a continuing dispute over whether it 
is an ancient object or a modern forgery.[6][10] The ring is commonly reported 
as having been allegedly discovered in a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos, 
but the available evidence does not provide a professional excavation record or
a secure chain of custody for that claim.[10]\n\nArthur Evans privately 
acquired the object and published it in 1925, interpreting the bezel scene as a
Minoan afterlife or Elysian domain in which a human couple enters the Elysian 
Fields.[10] A later interpretation identifies Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” 
motif as a scorpion, understood in that reading as an apotropaic figure that 
kills the ring’s “Minoan Dragon.”[6] These readings are interpretations of the 
imagery rather than settled identifications of every figure or motif.\n\nThe 
authenticity question remains unresolved in the supplied literature. An 
Athenian archaeological board reportedly regarded the ring as a forgery made by
a modern artist before Evans purchased it.[10] A 2020 contribution by A. G. 
Vlachopoulos, an archaeologist writing on Aegean iconography, reports a 
reassessment of the Ring of Nestor and related Cretan–Mycenaean gold rings in 
favor of authenticity, drawing broadly on comparative imagery and study of 
craftsmanship.[6] At the same time, a 2011 review of CMS VI states that doubts 
about no. 277 persisted, so the object is best presented as contested rather 
than conclusively authenticated.[12]\n\n## Discovery and provenance\n\nThe 
reported archaeological origin of the Ring of Nestor is an alleged discovery in
a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos. The account places the ring’s first 
appearance in scholarly literature in 1925, but the wording itself marks the 
findspot as unverified and the supplied passage contains no excavation record, 
tomb number, associated inventory, or documented transfer from excavators to 
the modern market.[10] The term *gemma dubitanda* is relevant to this 
evidentiary status: in the usage documented by the supplied study, it denotes 
sealstones and signet rings presented as possible Aegean Bronze Age objects 
whose provenance or date is uncertain and whose alleged origins are not 
securely established.[10]\n\nKakovatos is an independently documented 
archaeological site with three tholos tombs excavated by Wilhelm Dörpfeld in 
two campaigns, in 1907 and 1908.[15] The available Kakovatos study records gold
and other objects among the finds, but it does not identify an excavated object
from those tombs as the Ring of Nestor or as CMS VI, no. 277.[15] The site’s 
documented excavation history therefore confirms that Kakovatos is an 
archaeological locality, not that the particular ring came from a controlled 
excavation there.\n\nThe secure modern publication trail begins with Arthur 
Evans. The supplied account states that Evans privately purchased the object 
and published it in the *Journal of Hellenic Studies* in 1925.[10] His related 
1925 publication was also issued separately in London by Macmillan; the catalog
record describes a 75-page work with five plates and notes that it was 
published by permission from an April 1925 *Journal of Hellenic Studies* 
paper.[9] These bibliographic and acquisition details document the object’s 
Evans-era appearance in scholarship, but they do not supply the seller, earlier
owner, purchase documentation, or a complete chain of custody.[10][9]\n\nA 
separate treasure account must not be silently merged with the Kakovatos 
report. Evans’s 1925 work describes gold rings and metal bead-seals found in 
1915 in a rock-cut chamber tomb near Dombrena, by Thisbê in Boeotia, reportedly
discovered by a peasant under wartime conditions.[9] That Thisbê/Dombrena 
assemblage is geographically and contextually distinct from the alleged 
Kakovatos find, and the supplied material does not identify a specific object 
from it as CMS VI, no. 277.[9]\n\n## Description and iconography\n\nThe Ring of
Nestor is identified in the supplied scholarly discussion as a gold signet ring
and as CMS VI, no. 277.[6] The available evidence supports a broad description 
of a gold ring with a decorated bezel, but it does not provide authoritative 
measurements, a complete catalogue-style account of the hoop and bezel, or a 
specified manufacturing or surface-decoration technique for no. 277.[6][10][12]
A measurement of 28 × 17 mm appears in a cited note, but the antecedent of the 
pronoun is unavailable; assigning those dimensions to the Ring of Nestor would 
therefore risk confusing it with another object.[9]\n\nThe imagery is preserved
primarily through interpretation. Arthur Evans read the bezel as a Minoan 
Elysian or afterlife domain and described a human couple entering the Elysian 
Fields.[10] A reference note in the supplied material associates the ring with 
a seated goddess and a companion, but the referenced figure and surrounding 
description are not available in full.[9] A lower-credibility modern account 
calls the bezel depiction elaborate and characterizes it as an underworld 
scene; because that account does not provide a detailed image analysis or named
authorities, the description should be treated as a secondary summary rather 
than a definitive physical description.[8]\n\nA later iconographic proposal by 
A. G. Vlachopoulos, an archaeologist whose work concerns Aegean iconography, 
identifies the motif previously called Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” as a 
scorpion. In that interpretation the scorpion is an apotropaic symbol that 
kills the “Minoan Dragon” of the ring.[6] The accessible abstract does not 
include the image analysis supporting the identification, so the scorpion 
