of Nestor\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is the conventional name for a Cretan–Mycenaean
gold signet ring identified in the supplied scholarly literature as CMS VI, no.
277. Its modern importance derives from the combination of its elaborate bezel 
imagery, its association with Minoan and Mycenaean Bronze Age art, and a 
continuing dispute over whether it is an ancient object or a modern 
forgery.[6][10] The ring is commonly reported as having been allegedly 
discovered in a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos, but the available evidence
does not provide a professional excavation record or a secure chain of custody 
for that claim.[10]\n\nArthur Evans privately acquired the object and published
it in 1925, interpreting the bezel scene as a Minoan afterlife or Elysian 
domain in which a human couple enters the Elysian Fields.[10] A later 
interpretation identifies Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” motif as a scorpion, 
understood in that reading as an apotropaic figure that kills the ring’s 
“Minoan Dragon.”[6] These readings are interpretations of the imagery rather 
than settled identifications of every figure or motif.\n\nThe authenticity 
question remains unresolved in the supplied literature. An Athenian 
archaeological board reportedly regarded the ring as a forgery made by a modern
artist before Evans purchased it.[10] A 2020 contribution by A. G. 
Vlachopoulos, an archaeologist writing on Aegean iconography, reports a 
reassessment of the Ring of Nestor and related Cretan–Mycenaean gold rings in 
favor of authenticity, drawing broadly on comparative imagery and study of 
craftsmanship.[6] At the same time, a 2011 review of CMS VI states that doubts 
about no. 277 persisted, so the object is best presented as contested rather 
than conclusively authenticated.[12]\n\n## Discovery and provenance\n\nThe 
reported archaeological origin of the Ring of Nestor is an alleged discovery in
a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos. The account places the ring’s first 
appearance in scholarly literature in 1925, but the wording itself marks the 
findspot as unverified and the supplied passage contains no excavation record, 
tomb number, associated inventory, or documented transfer from excavators to 
the modern market.[10] The term *gemma dubitanda* is relevant to this 
evidentiary status: in the usage documented by the supplied study, it denotes 
sealstones and signet rings presented as possible Aegean Bronze Age objects 
whose provenance or date is uncertain and whose alleged origins are not 
securely established.[10]\n\nKakovatos is an independently documented 
archaeological site with three tholos tombs excavated by Wilhelm Dörpfeld in 
two campaigns, in 1907 and 1908.[15] The available Kakovatos study records gold
and other objects among the finds, but it does not identify an excavated object
from those tombs as the Ring of Nestor or as CMS VI, no. 277.[15] The site’s 
documented excavation history therefore confirms that Kakovatos is an 
archaeological locality, not that the particular ring came from a controlled 
excavation there.\n\nThe secure modern publication trail begins with Arthur 
Evans. The supplied account states that Evans privately purchased the object 
and published it in the *Journal of Hellenic Studies* in 1925.[10] His related 
1925 publication was also issued separately in London by Macmillan; the catalog
record describes a 75-page work with five plates and notes that it was 
published by permission from an April 1925 *Journal of Hellenic Studies* 
paper.[9] These bibliographic and acquisition details document the object’s 
Evans-era appearance in scholarship, but they do not supply the seller, earlier
owner, purchase documentation, or a complete chain of custody.[10][9]\n\nA 
separate treasure account must not be silently merged with the Kakovatos 
report. Evans’s 1925 work describes gold rings and metal bead-seals found in 
1915 in a rock-cut chamber tomb near Dombrena, by Thisbê in Boeotia, reportedly
discovered by a peasant under wartime conditions.[9] That Thisbê/Dombrena 
assemblage is geographically and contextually distinct from the alleged 
Kakovatos find, and the supplied material does not identify a specific object 
from it as CMS VI, no. 277.[9]\n\n## Description and iconography\n\nThe Ring of
Nestor is identified in the supplied scholarly discussion as a gold signet ring
and as CMS VI, no. 277.[6] The available evidence supports a broad description 
of a gold ring with a decorated bezel, but it does not provide authoritative 
measurements, a complete catalogue-style account of the hoop and bezel, or a 
specified manufacturing or surface-decoration technique for no. 277.[6][10][12]
A measurement of 28 × 17 mm appears in a cited note, but the antecedent of the 
pronoun is unavailable; assigning those dimensions to the Ring of Nestor would 
therefore risk confusing it with another object.[9]\n\nThe imagery is preserved
primarily through interpretation. Arthur Evans read the bezel as a Minoan 
Elysian or afterlife domain and described a human couple entering the Elysian 
Fields.[10] A reference note in the supplied material associates the ring with 
a seated goddess and a companion, but the referenced figure and surrounding 
description are not available in full.[9] A lower-credibility modern account 
calls the bezel depiction elaborate and characterizes it as an underworld 
scene; because that account does not provide a detailed image analysis or named
authorities, the description should be treated as a secondary summary rather 
than a definitive physical description.[8]\n\nA later iconographic proposal by 
A. G. Vlachopoulos, an archaeologist whose work concerns Aegean iconography, 
identifies the motif previously called Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” as a 
scorpion. In that interpretation the scorpion is an apotropaic symbol that 
kills the “Minoan Dragon” of the ring.[6] The accessible abstract does not 
include the image analysis supporting the identification, so the scorpion 
reading remains an attributed interpretation rather than an uncontested 
observation. The evidence likewise does not establish whether the bezel design 
should technically be called engraved, carved, raised, or relief 
work.[6][10]\n\nThe ring should also be distinguished from other gold signet 
rings. The four gold rings from the Griffin Warrior’s grave near Pylos, for 
example, are a separate group and are not identified in the supplied material 
as the Ring of Nestor.[8] Similarly, the references to Evans’s drawings, 
watercolours, and replicas, and to the separately named Ring of Minos, do not 
establish that those representations or objects are the original CMS VI no. 
277.[12]\n\n## Authenticity debate\n\nThe authenticity debate began with 
conflicting assessments of the object’s status. The supplied historical account
states that an archaeological board in Athens refused to buy the ring from its 
owner because its members considered it a forgery manufactured by a modern 
artist.[10] The same account says that Arthur Evans nevertheless privately 
purchased the object and accepted it as genuine, publishing it in 1925 and 
interpreting its bezel as a Minoan afterlife scene.[10] The board’s written 
report, examination procedure, and full list of participants are not supplied, 
so its judgment can be reported but not reconstructed in technical 
detail.\n\nThe principal objection concerns the absence of secure 
archaeological provenance. The category *gemma dubitanda* is used for 
sealstones and signet rings of alleged Bronze Age origin whose provenance and 
production date are unknown or unverified.[10] The supplied study states that 
such objects do not come from professional, well-documented excavations and 
that no decisive provenance reference was available for them.[10] Applied to 
the Ring of Nestor, this explains why the alleged Kakovatos findspot cannot by 
itself settle authenticity.\n\nArguments favoring authenticity have been 
renewed in later scholarship. A. G. Vlachopoulos’s 2020 contribution identifies
the object as the so-called Ring of Nestor, CMS VI, no. 277, and reports that 
earlier identifications of certain Cretan–Mycenaean gold rings as fakes were 
reassessed in favor of authenticity.[6] The abstract attributes that 
reassessment to comparisons with wall paintings, gold rings, figurines, and 
ivories, together with closer study of the techniques and craftsmanship of 
signet rings dated to the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age, the 16th–15th
centuries BC.[6] The captured abstract does not specify the individual 
comparative objects, laboratory procedures, or object-level results, so it 
records the direction of the argument without supplying its complete 
evidentiary demonstration.\n\nThe iconographic debate forms part of the same 
reassessment. Vlachopoulos identifies Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” as a 
scorpion and interprets it as an apotropaic figure that kills the “Minoan 
Dragon.”[6] Conversely, an abstract concerning Egyptian iconography reports 
arguments that the ring’s semantic coherence depends on Egyptian prototypes and
that its butterfly motif counts against authenticity.[7] Those arguments are 
available only in summary form in the supplied material and cannot be 
independently weighed here against the full comparative analysis.\n\nThe 
evidence therefore supports a historical conclusion rather than a definitive 
verdict. The ring has been accepted as authentic by Evans, rejected as a modern
forgery in the reported Athenian assessment, and defended in a later 
reassessment based on iconographic and craftsmanship comparisons.[10][6] A 2011
review of CMS VI nevertheless explicitly states that doubts about the 
authenticity of no. 277 remained, even while other previously suspect pieces 
were rehabilitated.[12] The Ring of Nestor consequently remains a contested 
object whose cataloguing identity and interpretive importance are clearer than 
its archaeological provenance or final authenticity status.\n\n## 
